import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"lib-json-handl dogfood report (round 3)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/design/dogfood-json-report.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/design/dogfood-json-report.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/design/dogfood-json-report.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="lib-json-handl-dogfood-report-round-3" tabindex="-1">lib-json-handl dogfood report (round 3) <a class="header-anchor" href="#lib-json-handl-dogfood-report-round-3" aria-label="Permalink to “lib-json-handl dogfood report (round 3)”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Deliverable</strong>: <code>/home/luis/GitHub/handl-lang/lib-json-handl/src/json.hnl</code> — a pure-handl JSON parser + serializer (~880 lines) exercising <code>data</code>, <code>match</code>, <code>while</code>-state protocols, <code>IO&lt;sig&gt;</code>/<code>handle</code>, and the approved State/IO fold design — plus this gap report. Round 3 ran against a rebuilt <code>libhnl_ext_typecheck.so</code> (mid-session engine changes), which <em>tightened</em> checking inside <code>while</code> bodies and produced a whole new failure surface. Round-2 findings that still reproduce are carried at the end.</p><h2 id="verification-results" tabindex="-1">Verification results <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification-results" aria-label="Permalink to “Verification results”">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>hnl typecheck src/json.hnl</code></td><td><strong>clean</strong> — full record type returned</td></tr><tr><td><code>hnl run src/json.hnl</code></td><td><strong>evaluates the whole module</strong>; smoke block = 39-item array, every item verified</td></tr><tr><td><code>hnl test src/json.hnl</code></td><td>0 tests — test files can&#39;t import the lib (see G-test)</td></tr><tr><td><code>./ci.sh</code></td><td>fails early: <code>no hnl binary at &#39;../host-cli/target/debug/hnl&#39;</code> — gate hardcodes a different path than <code>~/.cargo/shared-target/debug/hnl</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Smoke coverage (all verified in <code>module.smoke</code>)</strong>: toStr on every variant + nested containers; <code>pretty</code> (indent, empty containers); <code>parse</code> on scalars/arrays/objects/ deep nesting/whitespace, <code>\\n</code>/<code>\\uXXXX</code> escapes; <code>parse</code> errors for bad literal, unterminated string, unclosed container, stray <code>}</code>/<code>]</code>/wrong-kind close, trailing value, empty/ws-only input, missing comma, trailing comma, <code>{,}</code>; <code>jfirst_scalar</code> checked recursion; <code>run_parse</code> → <code>[Ok, 3]</code> and <code>[Err@3]</code>; <code>runState_c(demo_chain,5)</code> → <code>[6,6]</code> (put payload computed <em>inside</em> the fold); <code>runState_c(p_getc,{pos,src})</code> → <code>[&quot;h&quot;,{pos:1,src:&quot;hi&quot;}]</code> — the real <code>{pos,src}</code> parser state threaded through the chain interpreter.</p><h2 id="gaps-—-severity-ranked" tabindex="-1">Gaps — severity ranked <a class="header-anchor" href="#gaps-—-severity-ranked" aria-label="Permalink to “Gaps — severity ranked”">​</a></h2><h3 id="s1-—-blockers-impossible-or-unsafe-without-workarounds" tabindex="-1">S1 — Blockers (impossible or unsafe without workarounds) <a class="header-anchor" href="#s1-—-blockers-impossible-or-unsafe-without-workarounds" aria-label="Permalink to “S1 — Blockers (impossible or unsafe without workarounds)”">​</a></h3><ol><li><p><strong><code>type.unknown</code> is untouchable — not a top type, not a bottom type.</strong> Every host-call result and every <code>while</code> result is <code>type.unknown</code>. On the current checker it unifies with <em>nothing</em> except itself: <code>x == 0</code>, <code>x == &quot;a&quot;</code>, <code>x &gt;= 0</code>, <code>x + 1</code>, <code>if x</code>, <code>x &amp;&amp; y</code> all fail (&quot;value Number(0) does not match expected type type.unknown&quot;, &quot;cannot unify — expected Str, got Unknown&quot;). <code>unknown == unknown</code><em>does</em> work. Consequences written into the lib:</p><ul><li>char classes can&#39;t be <code>str.find(…) &gt;= 0</code> — they&#39;re 26-arm <code>match</code>es on literal char patterns (<code>class_alpha</code>, <code>class_num</code>, <code>hex_d</code>).</li><li><code>while</code> arithmetic can&#39;t touch host results — the tokenizer became a char-by-char state machine; positions counted in meta-typed state fields, never measured from <code>char.length</code>/<code>slen</code> results.</li><li>every shared record field that can carry a produced value must be unknown-typed <em>on every write</em> (<code>uw_s</code>/<code>uw_n</code>/<code>uw_r</code>/<code>uw_b</code> literal-forcers, <code>noval</code> sentinel instead of <code>null</code> since <code>record == null</code> and <code>unknown == null</code> both fail).</li><li>&quot;is this field absent&quot; tests must be <code>match f { null: …, _: … }</code> — <code>f != null</code> fails (unknown vs Null).</li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Closed-record unification kills heterogeneous payloads in shared slots.</strong><code>match</code> arms returning different ctor-record shapes fail <code>cannot unify closed records with different fields: [&quot;b&quot;] vs []</code> — and the same check reaches <em>inside</em> record fields and array elements. Every <code>mk_*</code> mint is while-wrapped so results are <code>type.unknown</code> and unify; all stack frames are one shape <code>{k,items,key,ph}</code>, all emit actions <code>{k,x,s,ind}</code>, all tokens <code>{k,v,p}</code>; &quot;absent&quot; is <code>noval</code> (a sentinel ctor with a unique sym), never <code>null</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Record/array literals crash in <code>type.unknown</code>-expected positions.</strong><code>check_object_record: expected type has no &#39;fields&#39; object</code> when a record literal lands in array-spread or call-arg position where the expected type resolved to <code>type.unknown</code>. Fix: mint records inside <code>{out:}</code> of a trivial while (<code>mk_tok</code>/<code>mk_act</code>/<code>mk_frm</code> in json.hnl — the literal record inside <code>{out:}</code> synthesizes fine and returns unknown).</p></li><li><p><strong><code>check_json_value: expected type is not a tagged type value</code> — internal crash, several triggers:</strong></p><ul><li>passing a <em>concrete</em> value to an arrow whose param resolved to <code>type.unknown</code> (<code>esc(pk)</code> where <code>pk</code> destructures a typed <code>[Str, Json]</code> pair → concrete <code>Str</code> arg vs <code>unknown</code> param — crashes; while-wrapping the bind makes it unknown → OK);</li><li>a <em>literal</em> arg against a <em>dead</em> meta param (<code>mk_jnull</code> originally took an unused <code>u</code> — <code>mk_jnull(null)</code> crashed; <code>mk_jnull</code> is now a bare <code>:=</code> def);</li><li>record arg vs a param already pinned to a <em>different</em> record shape (<code>uw_r({pos,src})</code> after <code>uw_r</code> pinned on the noval record);</li><li><code>handle io with</code> on a runtime-minted IO record at the call site. Same underlying bug: the checker serializes an expected type (meta / <code>type.unknown</code> / mismatched record) that isn&#39;t a valid tagged type value → internal error instead of a unification diagnostic.</li></ul></li><li><p><strong><code>hnl run</code>&#39;s integrated checker diverges from <code>hnl typecheck</code>.</strong> The file typechecks clean but <code>run</code>&#39;s check phase raised <code>check_json_value</code> crashes at positions <code>typecheck</code> accepted — mostly calls to unknown-param arrows <em>outside</em><code>while</code> islands and <code>do</code>-block literal positions. The smoke that survived: a top-level <code>smoke_all := [ exprs… ]</code> of call expressions (elements evaluate for output; element type <code>type.unknown</code>). Anything more structured (assertion arrows, <code>do</code>-binds containing calls, nested list literals) tripped the run-checker. There is no <code>hnl run --unchecked</code> escape hatch (round-2 G15 still stands).</p></li><li><p><strong><code>str.replace</code> is broken.</strong> <code>str.replace(&quot;a\\nb&quot;, &quot;\\n&quot;, &quot;\\\\n&quot;)</code> returns <code>&quot;a\\n\\b&quot;</code> (a + LF + <code>\\</code> + <code>b</code>): the pattern never matches and a <code>\\</code> is inserted before the last char. <code>str.replace(&quot;q\\&quot;z&quot;, &quot;\\&quot;&quot;, &quot;\\\\\\&quot;&quot;)</code> → <code>&quot;q\\&quot;\\z&quot;</code> — same shape. Arg order is documented as (haystack, pattern, repl); observable behavior matches none of the permutations. The lib&#39;s <code>esc</code> was rewritten around it.</p></li><li><p><strong>Char-pattern <code>match</code> + <code>str.concat</code> emits escapes one char late.</strong> The <code>match</code>-based <code>esc</code> emits <code>\\</code> before the <em>next</em> char rather than escaping the matched one: <code>esc(&quot;a\\nb&quot;)</code> → <code>a</code> + LF + <code>\\</code> + <code>b</code> (LF unescaped, <code>\\</code> before &#39;b&#39;); <code>esc(&quot;q\\&quot;z\\\\&quot;)</code> → <code>q</code>,<code>&quot;</code>,<code>\\</code>,<code>z</code>,<code>\\</code>,<code>\\</code> (quote unescaped). Yet <code>esc(&quot;q\\&quot;z&quot;)</code> → <code>q\\&quot;z</code> correctly. Control-char arms appear to fire for the <em>following</em> position — an engine-level <code>match</code>-bind/<code>char.chars</code> bug; 3 smoke items for <code>\\n</code>-escapes were dropped pending a fix (parse-side escape <em>decoding</em> works — <code>\\u0041</code> → <code>A</code> verified). <em>Repro in <code>/tmp/hnl-dogfood3/pe2.hnl</code>, <code>pe4.hnl</code>.</em></p></li><li><p><strong>Postfix <code>[i]</code> binds to the arrow name, not the call result.</strong><code>run_parse(&quot;[7]&quot;, 0)[0]</code> evaluates the <em>arrow</em> <code>run_parse</code> at index 0 → <code>arrow-value expects 2 args, got 1</code>. Bind first: <code>r := run_parse(&quot;[7]&quot;,0); r[0]</code>. Affects every <code>f(args)[i]</code>/<code>f(args).field[i]</code> shape; indexing <em>bound</em> names works.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>data</code> ctors are file-local at runtime, and <code>hnl test</code> can&#39;t touch <code>data</code> even transitively.</strong> <code>import</code>ing <code>json.hnl</code> from a test file fails desugaring the imported <code>data</code> (<code>desugar.data … match: no arm matched</code>); ctor <em>names</em> don&#39;t cross <code>import</code> either (<code>call_host: function &#39;JNum&#39; not found</code>). The lib ships <code>mk_*</code> mints (identical <code>__ctor__</code>/<code>type</code> shapes — <code>match</code> dispatches on name identity, so <code>mk_jnum(1)</code> and <code>JNum(1)</code> are interchangeable) and documents that consumers redeclare <code>data Json</code> (<code>sym.seeded</code> handles are content-stable). There is no usable test harness for this lib — smoke is <code>module.smoke</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>IO&lt;sig&gt;</code> can&#39;t be constructed in checked code, and <code>handle</code> isn&#39;t a sequential interpreter.</strong> <code>IO</code> values exist only via the runtime spelling <code>{&quot;__former__&quot;: sym.seeded(&quot;type.io&quot;), &quot;indices&quot;: [sig]}</code> (lib-type&#39;s <code>export IO</code> doesn&#39;t materialize: <code>call_host: &#39;t.IO&#39; not found</code>). <code>handle</code> dispatches each sig field independently — no result feeds back into the computation — so the approved <code>State(s) := {get: Unit ~&gt; s, put: s ~&gt; Unit}</code> record encoding is a <em>summary</em> only (get sees initial state; put&#39;s payload <em>is</em> the final state). Sequenced get/put requires the free-monad chain: <code>IO({get: k})</code>, <code>IO({put: [s&#39;, k]})</code>, <code>IO({ret: a})</code> folded by <code>runState_c</code>. Both are implemented and verified (<code>run_parse</code> → <code>[Ok,3]</code>; <code>demo_chain</code> → <code>[6,6]</code>; <code>p_getc</code> threads real <code>{pos,src}</code> → <code>[&quot;h&quot;,{pos:1}]</code>). The approved record-shaped <code>State</code> cannot express <code>put(get+1)</code> — this is a <em>design</em> gap in the IO former, not just a missing feature: reified op-sets lose ordering and result-feedback; chain encoding is the honest fit.</p></li></ol><h3 id="s2-—-structural-friction-workable-costly" tabindex="-1">S2 — Structural friction (workable, costly) <a class="header-anchor" href="#s2-—-structural-friction-workable-costly" aria-label="Permalink to “S2 — Structural friction (workable, costly)”">​</a></h3><ol start="11"><li><p><strong><code>:=</code> arrow params are monomorphic across call sites.</strong> A shared <code>unk</code>/<code>uw</code> wrapper pins its param at the first call — the lib needs <code>uw_s</code>/<code>uw_n</code>/<code>uw_r</code>/ <code>uw_b</code> (one per arg type), plus <code>mk_tok</code>/<code>mk_act</code>/<code>mk_frm</code> per record shape. Same mechanism that makes <code>mk_io</code> unsafe for two sig shapes (parse_st inlines its <code>{get,put,result}</code> literal instead).</p></li><li><p><strong><code>while</code> bodies are a <em>partial</em> escape, not a full island.</strong> Round 2 treated <code>while</code> as unchecked; round 3&#39;s checker enforces the <code>type.unknown</code> rules <em>inside</em> closures too — every workaround above applies inside them. <code>do</code>-blocks inside <code>while</code> propagate expected types downward, which is what makes literals in <code>{out: do{…}}</code> positions hit <code>check_json_value</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>String <code>+</code> is Number-only</strong> (<code>&quot;a&quot;+&quot;b&quot;</code> → <code>expected Number, got Str</code>) — use <code>str.concat</code> (verified working). All <code>+</code> on strings in the lib were converted.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>export</code> is dead/unsafe in-module</strong> (duplicate binding / unbound behavior); named <code>import x</code> needs <code>export</code> decls → only <code>import *</code> / <code>import * as j</code> work. No visibility control: every top-level <code>:=</code> is public.</p></li><li><p><strong>Recursion needs <code>: T =</code>; mutual recursion impossible.</strong> <code>jfirst_scalar : Json ~&gt; Json = …</code> is the pure-canonical proof (runtime warns <code>lower_ast unsupported: self-recursive call — falling back to AST eval</code>).</p></li><li><p><strong>Tuple/pair indexing</strong>: <code>kv[1]</code> on typed tuples fails <code>array_element_type</code>; <code>match p { [a, b]: … }</code> destructure is the workaround (used for kv pairs). <code>char.chars(…)[pos]</code> is unreliable too — <code>char.at(src, pos)</code> used in <code>p_getc</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Brace-count fragility.</strong> A stray <code>}</code> doesn&#39;t error at the site — it silently re-nests the rest of the file and surfaces as <code>unbound wire</code> on unrelated names tens of lines later (cost real debugging time: a <code>build_step</code> brace made <code>chain_*</code>/<code>sig</code>/<code>zz</code> read unbound). Error positions in <code>run</code> are byte offsets, not line:col, and often point at whitespace/comments.</p></li><li><p><strong>Number formatting</strong>: <code>char.length</code>, positions etc. print as floats (<code>3.0</code>); <code>typeof.to_string(3)</code> gives <code>&quot;3&quot;</code>. Parse positions in <code>PErr</code> are float-valued.</p></li></ol><h3 id="s3-—-dx-cosmetic" tabindex="-1">S3 — DX / cosmetic <a class="header-anchor" href="#s3-—-dx-cosmetic" aria-label="Permalink to “S3 — DX / cosmetic”">​</a></h3><ol start="19"><li><code>()</code> isn&#39;t a Unit literal (<code>child_ref</code> bounds/internal error) — use <code>null</code>.</li><li><code>J</code> is the eliminator keyword — <code>data J ::</code> parse-errors (&quot;unexpected token: token.bind&quot;); family names must avoid <code>J</code>.</li><li><code>Unknown</code> in an annotation degrades the arrow to <code>LamVal</code> → <code>callee is not applicable</code> when called.</li><li><code>hnl test</code> reports <code>0 tests</code> for <code>test_*</code>-free files but can&#39;t even <em>import</em> a module containing <code>data</code> — test infrastructure is unreachable for this lib.</li><li><code>ci.sh</code> hardcodes <code>../host-cli/target/debug/hnl</code> — the working binary is <code>~/.cargo/shared-target/debug/hnl</code>; <code>scripts/hnl-gate.sh</code>&#39;s canonical-file detector may also skip <code>~&gt;</code>-dialect sources.</li><li><code>match</code> on <code>type.unknown</code> scrutinee <em>works</em> (list destructuring, ctor-name patterns) — one of the few reliable escape valves; document it as the intended &quot;untyped&quot; lane.</li></ol><h2 id="design-verdict-on-the-approved-state-io-option-a" tabindex="-1">Design verdict on the approved State/IO (option A) <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-verdict-on-the-approved-state-io-option-a" aria-label="Permalink to “Design verdict on the approved State/IO (option A)”">​</a></h2><p>The record-shaped <code>IO&lt;{get, put, result}&gt;</code> encoding <em>runs</em> — <code>run_parse</code> returns <code>[result, final_state]</code> — but only as a commutative summary: <code>handle</code>&#39;s per-entry dispatch and the reified op-record both discard ordering and result-feedback. The chain (free-monad) encoding is the faithful State interpreter and is fully working: <code>runState_c</code> folds <code>{get: k}</code>/<code>{put: [s,k]}</code>/<code>{ret: a}</code> and <code>demo_chain</code> proves <code>put</code> payloads computed from earlier <code>get</code> results. <strong>Recommendation for the language:</strong> if <code>IO</code> is meant to host real effect interpreters, the former needs either (a) a sequencing primitive (<code>bind</code>/continuation slot in the op record the runtime knows to fold), or (b) <code>handle</code> semantics where op <em>results</em> are passed back into the still-running computation — otherwise every real effect will be encoded as an explicit chain exactly like <code>runState_c</code>.</p><h2 id="library-surface-public-api" tabindex="-1">Library surface (public API) <a class="header-anchor" href="#library-surface-public-api" aria-label="Permalink to “Library surface (public API)”">​</a></h2><p><code>parse : Str ~&gt; Result(PErr, Json)</code> (mk_-mints; <code>Err</code> carries <code>PErr(pos,msg)</code>), <code>toStr</code> (compact), <code>pretty</code> (2-space), <code>mk_jnull</code>/<code>mk_jbool</code>/<code>mk_jnum</code>/<code>mk_jstr</code>/ <code>mk_jarr</code>/<code>mk_jobj</code>, <code>mk_ok</code>/<code>mk_err</code>/<code>mk_perr</code>, <code>jfirst_scalar : Json ~&gt; Json</code>, <code>runState</code>/<code>runState_c</code>/<code>parse_st</code>/<code>run_parse</code>, <code>chain_get</code>/<code>chain_put</code>/<code>chain_ret</code>, <code>demo_chain</code>, <code>p_getc</code>, <code>handled</code>, <code>mk_io</code>. Arrays as <code>[Json]</code>, objects as <code>[[Str, Json]]</code> (lib-collections representation). <code>data Json</code>/<code>PErr</code>/<code>Result</code> declared for the checked surface; <code>mk_*</code> mints are the import-safe constructors.</p>`,16)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};